# Git attributes for HAFiscal

# Ensure shell scripts always use LF (Unix) line endings
# This is critical for cross-platform compatibility (Linux, macOS, WSL2)
# Note: Execute permissions must be set via 'git update-index --chmod=+x' for scripts
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# LaTeX files use LF
*.tex text eol=lf
*.bib text eol=lf
*.bst text eol=lf
*.cls text eol=lf
*.sty text eol=lf

# Markdown files use LF
*.md text eol=lf

# Configuration files use LF
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.cfg text eol=lf
*.ini text eol=lf

# Scripts without extension use LF
reproduce text eol=lf
reproduce_min text eol=lf

# Generated LaTeX artifacts (added 2026-08-02)
# The 22 tracked *.dep files are snapshot-package dependency manifests, rewritten by
# every build — and differently by each toolchain (pdfTeX vs tex4ht). With two sessions
# committing to this branch from different machines they collide constantly, and being
# TEXT git tries to line-merge them into nonsense. Auto-resolve instead: the content is
# regenerated by the next build, so either side is equally valid.
#
# REQUIRES a one-time per-clone config, as .gitattributes cannot ship a merge driver
# and git has NO built-in 'ours' driver (without this it silently falls back to a text
# merge and writes conflict markers):
#     git config merge.ours.driver true
#
# Note the rebase asymmetry: during `git pull --rebase` (pull.rebase=true here) "ours"
# is UPSTREAM, so the incoming version wins and a commit touching only *.dep may be
# dropped as "already upstream". Harmless for regenerated files; do not extend this
# pattern to hand-edited ones.
*.dep merge=ours

# Binary files
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.eps binary
*.svg binary

# Symlink protection
# Files in images/ directory should always be stored as symlinks, never as regular files
# This prevents accidental conversion on systems with limited symlink support
images/* symlink
